## Model routing

This section is here because a **model gateway is configured for this machine** (Services → Model gateway in the launcher). It would be absent otherwise — a project with no gateway reaches only Anthropic models, and routing advice for models you cannot call is noise. Turn it off per-project from the same card.

**The rule that outranks the table: a model name must name the model that answers.** Never point a Claude tier slot (`ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_{OPUS,SONNET,HAIKU,FABLE}_MODEL`, `ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL`) or the subagent slot (`CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`) at a vendor model. Dispatching under one name and answering with another makes every later "which model wrote this?" unanswerable, and it is why the gateway namespaces vendor ids as `claude-gw/<id>` instead of relabelling them. VCO never writes any of those slots — not even when it points the VS Code panel at the gateway; an override already present in your settings is preserved and reported, never silently honoured (v0.2.92: the ONE permitted edit is appending the `[1m]` context-window hint to the SAME model id, which is disclosed in the done message — the model a slot names is never changed). A subagent that declares no model runs on the session's model unless `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL` is set in the environment — check with `echo $CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL` (empty = inherits) rather than assuming. Choose models visibly, in `/model` or in the agent's own frontmatter.

**When you route to GLM, route to `claude-gw/glm-5.3`** — not the flash variant, and not a pre-5.3 version, unless the user explicitly asks for one. Version matters beyond quality: context windows are version-keyed (glm-5.2 is 1M, glm-5.1 is 200K), so "the GLM one" is not a specification.

⚠️ **Everything below is conditional on YOU having supplied the vendor's API
key.** VCO ships no third-party key and never writes one. Without a key and a
gateway, the `claude-gw/*` ids are unreachable and only the Anthropic rows apply.

**The capability ladder, and it is one ordering, not five opinions**:
**Fable 5.1 > Opus 5 > `glm-5.3` > `glm-5.3-flash` > Sonnet 5.** That is the
Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index v4.1.1 composite read in order — 66, 63,
60, 57, 55 — and it matches hands-on use on a large repository. Two consequences
worth stating because they surprise people: **both GLM tiers rank above Sonnet
5**, and `glm-5.3` sits nearer Opus than Sonnet. So **Sonnet should be a rare
choice** — reach for it when the models above it are unavailable, not as the
default middle rung.

| Task class | Route to | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Final plans, adversarial review, hardest architecture | Fable 5.1 | Highest independent composite (Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index v4.1.1: 66, top of the five compared) |
| Agentic coding on real repos, multi-file refactors | Opus 5 | 97.0% SWE-bench Verified, first of 86 models on that leaderboard (Vals AI) |
| Bulk code generation, debugging, mid-tier implementation | `claude-gw/glm-5.3` | Sits between Sonnet 5 and Opus 5 and **nearer Opus** — slightly below it. Roughly 60% of Sonnet's price |
| High-volume mechanical sweeps, first-pass triage | `claude-gw/glm-5.3-flash`, **behind a verifier** | **Slightly above Sonnet 5**, at ~1/15 the price |
| Anything with an image, screenshot or diagram as input | a Claude model | GLM-5.3 (non-flash) is text-only |
| Unsupervised edits to a green test suite | **not flash** | It broke an already-passing baseline test in 6.9% of agent rollouts (vs 4.4% for GLM-5.3) |

**Where those placements come from, and which evidence wins.** The two GLM rows
reflect hands-on use on a large real codebase, not a leaderboard: `glm-5.3` reads
as an Opus-class model a little short of Opus, and flash as a little better than
Sonnet 5. A published composite (Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index v4.1.1)
scores them 60 and 57 against Sonnet 5's 55 — same ordering, but it compresses a
gap that is wider in practice. Where the two disagree, **prefer the hands-on
placement**: no public index measures agentic work on a repository of this size,
and the index figures have no in-repo citation, while the placement above does.

Two guardrails on flash, both from the same measurement: retries rescue it, more effort does not (longer runs helped it only 46% of the time — below chance), so cascade rather than escalate, and always verify its output against tests or a stronger second pass.

**Do not propagate the circulating "GLM-5.3-Flash = 92% SWE-bench Verified" figure.** It is not on the leaderboard it cites and contradicts every other measurement of that model.

**Window size is known. Competence across it is not. Keep the two apart.**

*Capacity* is settled data, version-keyed and cited — never guess it and never
infer it from a family name. The shipped table
(`claude_mcp_servers/model_router/chat_model_context.seed.json`, one row per FULL
model id, each with its vendor citation) is the source of truth: `glm-5.3`,
`glm-5.3-flash` and `glm-5.2` are 1M/128K; `glm-5.1`, `glm-5`, `glm-5-turbo`,
`glm-4.7` and `glm-4.6` are 200K/128K; `glm-4.5` and `glm-4.5-air` are 128K/96K.
So `glm-5.2` has **five times** the window of `glm-5.1` — which is exactly why a
`glm-5*` wildcard is forbidden. Claude models are deliberately absent from that
table: the client already knows their windows natively, and overriding them
would only introduce drift.

*Competence* across a large window is the open question: *"1M tokens accepted"*
is a capacity claim, not a quality one, and no vendor here publishes
independently-verified retrieval or reasoning quality at the top of its range.
Do not pick a model for long-context work on window size alone — but do not
treat the window itself as unknown, because it is not.

